3.3. Principles of UI design
============================
Principles of UI Design
=======================
1. Clarity
==========
The interface should be easy to understand.
Buttons, icons, and menus should clearly show their purpose.
Users should know what to do without confusion.

Example:
=======
A trash icon for delete
A search icon for search

2. Consistency
==============
Use the same styles and behaviors throughout the interface.
Same button colors
Same font styles
Same navigation patterns

Consistency helps users learn faster.

Example:
========
All “Save” buttons should look identical.

3. Simplicity
=============
Keep the design clean and avoid unnecessary elements.

Remove clutter
Use minimal text
Focus on important actions

Simple interfaces improve usability.

4. Feedback
===========
The system should respond to user actions.

Examples:
=========
Button changes color when clicked
Loading spinner appears (A loading spinner is a visual animation that tells users the system is 
processing something or waiting for data to load)
Success message after saving

Feedback tells users what is happening.

5. Visibility
=============
Important elements should be easy to find.

Menus should be visible
Important buttons should stand out
Information hierarchy should be clear

Example:
========
“Login” button highlighted prominently

6. Accessibility
================
Design should be usable by everyone, including people with disabilities.

Examples:
=========
Large readable text
Good color contrast
Keyboard navigation
Screen reader support

Accessibility improves usability for all users.

7. User Control
===============
Users should feel in control of the interface.

Examples:
=========
Undo button
Back button
Cancel option

Avoid forcing users into unwanted actions.

8. Efficiency
=============
Tasks should be completed quickly and easily.

Examples:
=========
Short navigation paths
Keyboard shortcuts
Auto-complete search

Efficient interfaces save time.

9. Visual Hierarchy
===================
Arrange elements according to importance.

Methods:
========
Larger text for headings
Bold important buttons
Use spacing and colors wisely

This guides user attention.

10. Error Prevention and Recovery
==================================
Help users avoid mistakes and recover easily.

Examples:
=========
Confirmation before deleting
Form validation (Form validation is the process of checking whether the data entered into a form 
is correct, complete, and acceptable before submission, it helps prevent errors and improves user
experience)
Helpful error messages

Good UI reduces frustration.


